      "title": "Restore two workgroups \u00b7 development fixture",
      "summary": "Review an access port while retaining two working groups.",
      "objective": "Staff workstations must exchange broadcasts with each other in VLAN 10. Guests must exchange broadcasts in VLAN 20 and stay separate from staff. Keep the equipment connected. Inspect the port settings, repair the fault and check both service and isolation.",
      "difficulty": "Beginner",
      "minutes": 12,
      "steps": [
        "Inspect group membership and repair the access port."
      ],
      "guide": [],
      "explanation": "The workstation sends untagged data. Its access port selects the local VLAN; the trunk carries that VLAN to the far switch. An access port in the wrong VLAN joins the wrong group. Service within both groups matters as well as separation.",
      "hints": [
        "Compare the access VLAN at the two staff ports.",
        "A workstation uses an access port. Trunks carry multiple VLANs between switches."
      ],
      "sources": [
        "https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/td/docs/switches/lan/catalyst9300/software/release/17-18/configuration_guide/vlan/b_1718_vlan_9300_cg/configuring_vlan_trunks.html"
      ],
      "simplifications": "Development fixture, not added curriculum. Broadcast data plane only; no ping, MAC learning, STP/PVST or Cisco command interpreter. Plain workstations send and receive untagged frames.",
